INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Senate Select Committee Intelligence Volume II", "source_url": "https://www.intelligence.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/sites-default-files-documents-report-volume2.pdf", "text": "SRC-003: The Senate report states that in 2016 St. Petersburg-based IRA operatives conducted an information-warfare campaign using targeted ads, falsified news, self-generated content, and platform tools while masquerading as Americans, attempting to deceive tens of millions. It found the campaign sought to polarize Americans along societal, ideological, and racial differences, provoke real-world events, and covertly support Russia's favored presidential candidate. The Committee found the IRA sought to harm Hillary Clinton and support Donald Trump at Kremlin direction; Russian government tasked and supported the IRA, and Prigozhin's direction/financial involvement and ties to senior Russian officials pointed to Kremlin support, authorization, and direction. It found broader objectives beyond the election: undermine public faith in U.S. democracy, exploit race, immigration, Second Amendment and other divisions, stoke anger, outrage/protest, and distrust in government. African-Americans were the most targeted group; over 66% of IRA Facebook advertisements contained a race-related term; Blacktivist generated 11.2 million Facebook engagements; 96% of IRA YouTube content targeted racial issues/police brutality. Paid ads were not key to overall activity: about $100,000 in Facebook ads over two years versus approximately $1.25m monthly operating costs; about 3,400 Facebook/Instagram ads versus 61,500 Facebook posts, 116,000 Instagram posts, and 10.4m tweets. The Committee found the IRA coopted unwitting Americans for petitions, personal information, self-defense courses, and rallies; it had some interaction with Trump Campaign for rally materials/organization. Committee warned full scope remains unknown because it relied substantially on platforms to identify activity."}
